00:00:06.550 --> 00:00:08.150 align:start position:0%
guns are one of mankind's most ingenious  and deadly inventions  from standard issue handguns to fearsome  beasts like the ones we're about to see  guns have been a wartime staple since  their inception in the 13th century  while most firearms follow a practical  design scheme over the course of the  last several hundred years there have  been some eccentric outliers  in this video we're going to look at  some of the largest most ridiculously  oversized guns in history  let's start with the oldest weapon on  our list the dardanelle's gun  while there were a handful of cannons  technically larger than this one  unlike the dardanelles gun very few of  them were ever fired in anger  the massive cannon was cast out of  bronze way back in 1464 in modern day  turkey  it weighed a staggering 16.8 tons and  was over 5 meters long  the projectiles for this beast were  large stone balls up to 63 centimeters  in diameter  the dardanelle's gun looks impressive  enough on paper but what really makes it  exceptional is that in 1807  340 years after it was built it was  dusted off and pressed back into service  to fend off the british royal navy in  what became known as the dardanelles  operation  the british suffered 28 dead from the  old cannons bombardment  60 years later it was gifted to queen  victoria and it now resides at fort  nelson in hampshire  for those of you who want the absolute  biggest medieval artillery piece and  aren't concerned with its military usage  up for your consideration is the tsar  cannon like the dardanelle's gun this  behemoth was cast in bronze  it was 5.3 meters long and weighed more  than twice the dardanelle's gun a  whopping 39.3 tons  as you might have guessed from its name  the tsar cannon hails from russia  specifically it was crafted in moscow in  1586 by andrei chokov  officially this imposing piece was never  meant to see combat  but was instead designed as a symbol of  russian power and industry  however at least one study of the tsar  cannon has revealed gunpowder residue  which would seem to indicate that it was  fired at least once perhaps  simply a ceremonial puff of smoke  contrary to what its current display  would have you believe  the tsar cannon was only meant to fire  800 kilograms of stone grape shot rather  than solid cannonballs  this earned it the nickname the russian  shotgun to this day  the tsar cannon holds the record for the  largest muzzle loading cannon ever built  moving forward to world war 1 we get a  taste of truly ridiculous scale  between march and august of 1918 the  german empire fielded what became known  as the paris gun  this unprecedented siege weapon had a  barrel length of 34 meters and holds the  prestigious honor of launching the  projectile that became the first  man-made object to enter the  stratosphere  a height not exceeded until the germans  developed their v2 rockets during world  war ii  you heard that right the paris gun was  capable of firing 106 kilogram shells  40 kilometers into the air that's almost  four  times the maximum altitude of today's  commercial airliners  this massive power allowed the paris gun  to bombard targets over 130 kilometers  away  the altitude also had another  complicating effect the flight time per  shell was about three minutes  so long that the germans had to factor  in the rotation of the earth to even hit  the city  when the germans began their barrage of  paris the citizens initially thought  they were under attack from a  high-flying  zeppelin because they could neither hear  an airplane nor a cannon of course  there was a cannon it was just too far  away to here  by the end of its service the paris gun  had fired between 320 and 367 shells and  killed 250 people and wounded an  additional 620 with a significant  portion of the death toll coming from a  single lucky shot on a crowded church  the gun was rushed back to germany as  the allies advanced where it was  dismantled and all schematics presumably  destroyed  jumping forward again to world war ii we  find the germans back at it again with a  new monster weapon  germany thought they would need a way to  break through the french maginot line so  they commissioned an unprecedented  weapon with a shell that could punch  through seven meters of concrete or one  meter of steel armor plating from beyond  the range of the french artillery  an engineer working on the project  calculated that such a feat would  require a weapon with a caliber of 80  centimeters that would weigh over 1 000  tons  supported by not one but two railway  tracks with a projectile weighing seven  tons  as much as a bus so naturally hitler  ordered two of them  the design and construction work got  underway but it soon became apparent  that the guns would not be ready in time  however the german army quickly flanked  the static maginot line through belgium  and its destruction became unnecessary  the first completed gun nicknamed gustav  was eventually deployed at the siege of  sevastopol in the soviet union  where it caused considerable damage with  its massive bust weight shells  the finished gun had a barrel over 30  meters long weighed 1  350 tons and could lob shells nearly 40  kilometers  the massive artillery piece required an  equally massive crew  needing 250 men just to remain in  operation and continue its rate of fire  of one to two shots per hour  besides the men required to operate the  behemoth the gustav gun also called for  1250 engineers  scientists and guards to assemble it  over the course of the four weeks  following its arrival at the front  aboard 25 separate trains it may have  been a marvel of engineering but it was  a logistical nightmare  gustav and its sister gun dora were both  dismantled by the germans near the end  of the war  to this day the gustav railway gun  remains the largest caliber rifled  weapon ever used in combat  the heaviest mobile artillery piece ever  built and fired the heaviest shells of  any artillery piece ever made  and finally the last gun on our list was  never actually finished  but if it had been it would have been  the largest gun ever made  it was called big babylon and was part  of an overarching plan by iraqi  president saddam hussein to create  several super guns  the project was headed by canadian  artillery expert gerald bull  bull had a tumultuous history with many  world powers spearheading the  development of advanced artillery  capable of launching satellites into  space  but also dabbling in illegal arms  dealing after a short stint in prison he  built a personal rapport with hussein  and convinced him that iraq needed such  advanced capabilities if it were ever to  become a regional power  construction began on what was called  baby babylon a smaller scale prototype  of the planned super gun  despite its reduced capabilities baby  babylon would have had a range of almost  650 kilometers  the real goal big babylon was planned to  be over 156 meters long  weigh 2 100 tons and have a bore of one  meter  this absurdly huge artillery piece would  have had the capability to fire two-ton  projectiles into orbit  where they could be guided to strike  anywhere on the planet when iraq invaded  kuwait in 1990  bull suddenly found himself working for  a man considered the enemy of almost  every nation on earth  in march of the same year bull was  assassinated with five shots to the back  of the neck as he entered his apartment  in brussels  there's much speculation regarding who  had bull assassinated  but the mystery has never been solved  regardless of the culprit  project babylon died with bull had it  been completed  big babylon would have been the most  ridiculously huge weapon ever devised  and it's doubtful if any such project  will ever be attempted again  if you'd like to learn more about the  history of super guns and other  artillery check out the links in the  description  if you enjoyed this video make sure to  subscribe to keep up to date with the  latest content  you can watch my previous video by  clicking here or watch my other videos  about weapons by clicking here  thanks for watching and i'll see you in  the next video
 

